  "final_span": "Kohli, who coordinates UTSA's Center for Experiential Learning, is overwhelmed with responses, including dozens of emails from The Washington Post and other entities, all regarding an unofficial notice posted by UTSA students last weekend.\nadministration approved last weekend, is reproduced almost word for word from a code at the California Polytechnic State University.\nKohli, 23, serves as the student coordinator for the office that encourages academic honesty. He claims unintentionally, or so he argues, that what was his original work now appears as an act of academic fraud, had he omitted the proper attribution to the California code in the what-might-have-been code.\nHe remains baffled by the lack of a credit line acknowledging his authorship of the honor code.\n\"It's a student initiative,\" Kohli said, \"We were trying to show a source when it comes to the penalties for violations.\"\nKohli removed the previous week's warning to ensure UTSA was not penalized for academic fraud as the university aimed to enforce a policy on \"integrity.\"\nSenior Director Thornton Lockwood of Clemson University's Center for Educational Technology believes Kohli is not at fault, but asserts that UTSA ought to have removed the badly sourced and plagiarized content. Furthermore, he advises caution regarding the Internet, given its abundant availability of snippets and beneficial information.\n\"We can cut and paste things that other people said,\" Lockwood warned as much of the world do, he said, \"when they shouldn't be.\"\nHe does, however, advise, \"to whom you cut and paste and at the bottom, put who you cut-and-pasted from and let the reader deal with the original author who said what that person says.\"\nSince UTSA has moved beyond its former honor code in 2013 and has no specific code to address academic fraud cases, such issues are managed using the university\u2019s existing policies, particularly those related to problematic plagiarism. The Cal Poly startup was a disruption at the student group, which intentionally marks Saturday as \"Honor Code Awareness",
